comments.body:
Reloading the extensions with [`session.defaultSession.loadExtension(path)`](https://<redacted-host>/docs/latest/api/session#sesloadextensionpath-options) after some delay seems to work(`1s` right after the `.installExtension([ext])` call).

Some thing like this(got hint form [here](https://github.com/MarshallOfSound/electron-devtools-installer/issues/244#issuecomment-2004456380))
```js
await require("node:timers/promises").setTimeout(1000);
session.defaultSession.getAllExtensions().map((ext) => {
  session.defaultSession.loadExtension(ext.path)
});
```

Here are the changes for working DevTools installation: https://github.com/usebruno/bruno/pull/3750/files#diff-f4f68ad80b8c2edfa98747336270c25acbc51531460feadd6bf496ca3314f854R55-R70

Without the reload with delay, the behavior seems inconsistent
- _Redux DevTools_ doesn't recognize the store during the first start-up
- _React Developer Tools_ loads(adds the Components and Profiler) only sometimes with `.loadExtension` **without** delay and never without it.

This issue occurs because [react developer tool's content script](https://github.com/facebook/react/blob/main/packages/react-devtools-extensions/src/contentScripts/prepareInjection.js) attempts to communicate with the background service worker, which isn't running on startup (after the first install).

A fix in Electron will likely be needed to launch these service workers upon startup. For now, you can upgrade to Electron v35 and use the following workaround:

```js
import { app, session as electronSession } from 'electron'
import { installExtension, updateExtensions } from 'electron-chrome-web-store'

function launchExtensionBackgroundWorkers(session = electronSession.defaultSession) {
  return Promise.all(
    session.getAllExtensions().map(async (extension) => {
      const manifest = extension.manifest;
      if (manifest.manifest_version === 3 && manifest?.background?.service_worker) {
        await session.serviceWorkers.startWorkerForScope(extension.url);
      }
    })
  );
}

app.whenReady().then(async () => {
  await installExtension('fmkadmapgofadopljbjfkapdkoienihi');
  await updateExtensions();
  await launchExtensionBackgroundWorkers();

  // launchMyApp();
});
```

> [@participant15](https://github.com/participant15) No extra info needed. As far as I know, [@participant14](https://github.com/participant14) is working on it.

@participant14 any updates or is the workaround of essentially loading the extension twice (or similar) still the best workaround today?

Thanks!
